  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to implement a probabilistic adaptive resolution approach to find the optimal balance between function complexity and computational efficiency. This method dynamically adjusts the resolution of the step function while maintaining the integral constraint, leveraging machine learning-inspired dimensionality reduction techniques. The approach aims to escape local minima by continuously evaluating the trade-off between representation accuracy and optimization speed, effectively balancing computation time against solution quality.\n\nKey innovations include:\n- Resolution adaptation based on evolutionary pressure from the optimization landscape\n- Dimensionality reduction via correlated clustering\n- Probabilistic phase transitions between low and high resolutions\n- Learnt decision boundaries to guide adaptation\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nRepresentation: Piecewise-constant approximation with variable $n = 2^k$ samples ($k \\in \\mathbb{N}$)\nObjective: $C_5 = \\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx$\n\nVariables: $h_i \\in [0,1]$ for $i=1,\\dots,n$\n\nConstraints:\na. $\\sum_{i=1}^n h_i \\frac{2}{n} = 1$\nb. $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1 \\forall i$\n\nParametrization:\nh is represented in two forms:\n- Low-resolution mode: $h^{\\text{low}} = \\text{round}(h^{\\text{full}} \\cdot 4) / 4$ \n- High-resolution mode: $h^{\\text{high}} = h^{\\text{full}}$\n\nTransition conditions:\n- Condition on payload variance $\\sigma_v^2$:\n  $\\sigma_v^2 = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_i (h_i - \\bar{h})^2$\n- Mode switch occurs at $\\eta_v \\in [0,1]$\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nInitialize:\n- Set $n_{\\max} = 600$ (even)\n- $X \\sim \\text{Uniform}([0,2])^n$\n- $h \\sim \\text{Beta}(2,2)$ (clipped to [0,1]) \n- $\\alpha_0 = 1.2$ (score multiplier factor)\n- $\\sigma_v^{\\text{target}} = 0.3$\n\nCore evolution loop:\n- Adaptive resolution selection: $k_{\\text{new}} \\leftarrow k_{\\text{old}} + \\epsilon \\cdot \\text{sign}(\\partial_n E)$\n- Phase transitions based on $\\sigma_v^2$:\n  - Low $\\sigma_v^2$: $\\mu_{\\text{step}} = 30$, $\\sigma_{\\text{step}} = 8$\n  - High $\\sigma_v^2$: $\\mu_{\\text{step}} = -30$, $\\sigma_{\\text{step}} = 8$\n- Candidate generation: Gaussian perturbations with learned mean shifts\n- Repair mechanism: projected gradient descent on integral constraint\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\na) Initial configuration:\n   - $n_0 = 100$\n   - Gaussian smoothing: $\\sigma = 0.02$\n   In $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n\nb) Mutation strategy:\n   - Adaptive covariance matrix:\n     $$\\mathbf{C}_{ij} \\leftarrow (1-\\rho)\\mathbf{C}_{ij} + \\rho\\delta_{ij}\\sigma^2$$\n   - with $\\rho=0.3$\n\nc) Decision rules:\n   - Maintain two candidate vectors $\\mathbf{h}_{(1)}, \\mathbf{h}_{(2)}$\n   - Gradient information:\n     $$\\nabla E \\propto \\frac{\\partial C_5}{\\partial h_i} = \\text{correlation}(1-h, \\delta_i)$$\n\nd) Phase switching (based on variance threshold $T$):\n   - If $\\sigma_v^2 < T$: switch to finer resolution\n   - If $\\sigma_v^2 > T$: switch to coarser resolution\n   - T variant every ${\\max(n,300)}$\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nTime estimation:\n- For $n$ samples $\\sim 8n$ flops per function evaluation\n- Assume 1000 evaluations at $n=100$ dominates total cost\n- Time: $\\sim 0.0001 \\cdot n^2$ seconds\n\nTermination:\n- $\\Delta E / E < 10^{-4}$ or time over\n- At $t>t_{\\text{terminate}}$: $n \\leftarrow \\text{opposite of mean gradient}$\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- Clip $h_i$ to [0,1]\n- Gradient projection repair integral: \n  $h_{\\text{proj}} = h \\cdot \\frac{\\text{target}}{\\sum h_i}$\n\nEvaluator compatibility:\n- Full gradient calculation to verify\n- 3-point Simpson to estimate $C_5$\n\nReporting:\n- Return $(h^{\\text{best}}, C_5^{\\text{best}}, n)$\n- If stuck: $n \\leftarrow \\floor{\\sqrt{n}}$",
